AI Document, Database & Voice Assistant
========================================

A multi-modal assistant that answers questions three ways from one chat
box: grounded in documents you upload, grounded in a real (read-only)
Postgres/Supabase database, or general knowledge from Google Gemini (with
an optional web-search toggle). Conversations persist per session, can be
continued by voice through a live Gemini connection, and answers can be
exported to Word/Excel.

Tech: React (Vite) + FastAPI + Google Gemini (google-genai) + Postgres/
      Supabase (psycopg2 + SQLAlchemy) + scikit-learn (in-memory document
      retrieval) + pypdf/pymupdf/python-docx + openpyxl (exports)
      + WebSockets (Gemini Live voice)

Answer modes (routed automatically per question by services/
intent_router.py, or combined when a question needs more than one):

  - DATABASE_QUERY    natural language -> SQL (services/db_query_service.py),
                       run read-only against your Postgres/Supabase database,
                       guarded by services/sql_guard.py (SELECT-only, table
                       allowlist, row cap, statement timeout).
  - PDF_QUERY          grounded in a file uploaded via POST /documents/upload
                       (PDF/DOCX/TXT, plus images via Gemini OCR), held in
                       memory only (services/document_store.py) and
                       retrieved with TF-IDF - nothing is written to disk,
                       nothing survives a restart.
  - GENERAL_KNOWLEDGE  plain Gemini, optionally grounded with a live web
                       search when "Web Search" is turned on in the chat box.
  - HYBRID_QUERY       a question needing more than one of the above
                       answered together (e.g. an uploaded PDF's numbers
                       compared against a DB table).

Every answer in a chat session is saved (services/chat_memory.py, in a
dedicated `chat_memory` Postgres schema kept isolated from your business
data/tables) so later questions in the same session can refer back to
earlier ones. Voice conversations ("Voice Chat" in the sidebar, backend
/ws/voice - a WebSocket to Gemini Live) reuse that same session memory
and the same DATABASE_QUERY/GENERAL_KNOWLEDGE logic as typed chat.


SETUP
-----

1. Backend environment

   cd backend, copy .env.example to .env, and fill in:

       GEMINI_API_KEY   - required. Free key at
                          https://aistudio.google.com/apikey

       SUPABASE_DB_URL  - optional; only needed for DATABASE_QUERY answers
       (or the discrete PGHOST/PGPORT/PGUSER/PGPASSWORD/PGDATABASE/
       PGSSLMODE vars instead). Use a dedicated READ-ONLY Postgres role,
       not a superuser.

       DB_QUERY_ALLOWED_TABLES - strongly recommended once a DB is
       connected, to limit which tables Gemini/SQL Guard can see.

   See the comments in backend/.env.example for every other optional
   knob (rate limiting, schema cache TTL, row limit, query timeout,
   voice pipeline debug logging).

2. Start the backend (Windows)

       rem 1. From the project root, create a venv for the backend
       cd backend
       py -3.12 -m venv venv

       rem 2. Activate it
       venv\Scripts\activate

       rem 3. Install dependencies
       pip install -r requirements.txt

       rem 4. Run the server
       uvicorn main:app --reload

   Runs on http://127.0.0.1:8000. Python 3.12 is what CI and the Render
   deployment both pin (backend/.python-version, render.yaml), so that is
   what local dev matches for reproducibility - but nothing in the app
   itself requires it anymore. (It used to: faster-whisper's ctranslate2
   dependency shipped a broken Windows wheel under 3.13. That dependency
   was removed when voice transcription moved to Gemini, so a 3.13 venv
   works fine too if that is what you already have installed - this repo
   is tested and passing under both.)

   venv/ and .env are both already excluded via .gitignore - never
   commit either one.

3. Start the frontend

       cd frontend
       npm install
       npm run dev

   Runs on http://localhost:5173. Optionally copy frontend/.env.example
   to frontend/.env if the backend is not reachable at the default
   http://127.0.0.1:8000.


RUNNING TESTS
-------------

Backend (pytest; Gemini and the database are mocked, so no live network
or DB connection is needed to run the suite):

       cd backend
       venv\Scripts\activate
       pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
       pytest -v

Frontend (Node's built-in test runner, covers frontend/src/utils/*.js):

       cd frontend
       npm test

Frontend lint + production build (what CI's frontend-build job runs):

       cd frontend
       npm run lint
       npm run build

.github/workflows/ci.yml runs the backend pytest step and both frontend
steps above on every push/PR to main - that workflow is the source of
truth for what "passing" means in this repo.


HOW TO USE
----------

1. Open http://localhost:5173
2. Optionally upload a PDF, DOCX, or TXT file (top bar) to add it to that
   chat's document context - each upload attaches to the current chat
   session only (not persisted across a page refresh) and replaces any
   document already attached to it; there is no shared knowledge base
   across sessions.
3. Type a question, or click the microphone to speak it, or open
   "Voice Chat" (sidebar) for a continuous live voice conversation.
4. Turn on "Web Search" in the chat box if a general-knowledge question
   needs current information beyond Gemini's training data.
5. Click the speaker icon under any answer to hear it read aloud.
6. Ask for an answer "as a Word document" / "as an Excel file" to get a
   downloadable export of it.


API
---

POST /chat                        ask a question; streams the answer back
                                   as NDJSON. See routes/chat.py's
                                   ChatRequest for every optional field
                                   (session_id, document_id, web_search,
                                   previous_question/previous_answer).
POST /documents/upload            attach a PDF/DOCX/TXT/image to a session.
DELETE /documents/{document_id}   drop that session's attached document.
POST /transcribe                  one-shot speech-to-text (chat box mic).
WS   /ws/voice                    continuous voice conversation via
                                   Gemini Live.
GET  /export/download/{export_id} download a generated DOCX/XLSX file.
GET  /health                      liveness check.


NOTES
-----

- PDF, DOCX, TXT, and common image formats are accepted; old .doc files
  are not. A scanned PDF with no text layer still works - it falls back
  to Gemini image understanding instead of being rejected.
- Uploaded documents are held in memory only (services/document_store.py)
  and expire after DOCUMENT_TTL_SECONDS (default 3600s / 1 hour) or a
  server restart - nothing is written to disk.
- Chunking/retrieval knobs: PDF_CHUNK_SIZE / PDF_CHUNK_OVERLAP
  (services/pdf_service.py, default 600/80 characters) and
  PDF_RETRIEVAL_TOP_K (services/pdf_retrieval.py, default 3 chunks).
- DATABASE_QUERY answers and chat memory both require SUPABASE_DB_URL/
  PG* to be set. Without them the app still works for documents and
  general knowledge - a database question gets an honest "no database is
  connected" answer instead of a fabricated one, and chat memory is
  simply not persisted between requests.
- This project has no authentication - anyone who can reach the backend
  can use it. services/rate_limiter.py (per-IP request throttling) is
  the only abuse guard in place; deploy behind your own access control
  if that matters for your use case.
- Two Gemini models matter: GEMINI_MODEL generates answers, and Gemini's
  own audio understanding (no separate embedding model needed anymore)
  handles transcription. If you ever see "model is not available for
  your key", list what your key can actually use:

      cd backend
      venv\Scripts\python.exe -c "import os;from dotenv import load_dotenv;from google import genai;load_dotenv();[print(m.name) for m in genai.Client(api_key=os.getenv('GEMINI_API_KEY')).models.list()]"
